  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current method uses a grid representation of the function h and applies local optimization methods. While it achieves a low value, it may be stuck in local minima. The key idea is to use coarse grid representations to escape these local minima and then refine the solution on finer grids. This multi-scale approach leverages the observation that the optimal h often has simple structure, such as clustering into intervals, which is easier to capture on coarser grids. We hypothesis that by optimizing a piecewise constant version of h on a small grid, the function will develop a structure that can be interpolated to the fine grid and further optimized. This adds two new phases: coarse grid interpolation and grid descent, complementing the existing steps.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Input: Step function h on [0,2] with integral 1 and entries in [0,1]\n- Output: Optimal h_values satisfying the constraints\n- Formulation: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constrained by: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1\n- Representation: Piecewise constant on N grid points, with grid spacing dx=2/N\n- Objective evaluation: For any candidate h, compute C_5 as max over k of dx times the autocorrelation of h and (1-h) evaluated at lag k\n\nImplementation Procedure (walkthrough):\n1. Initialize: \n   - If initial_h_values is available, use it on fine grid (N=400)\n   - Or initialize random h_values on fine grid\n\n2. CoarseGridPhase:\n   - For each grid size N_coarse in [128,256,512,768,1024] (in descending order):\n     a. Downsample candidate to N_coarse points\n     b. Filter candidate to only those with lower C_5 than best recorded\n     c. Optimize Schwarz algorithm (Kushnick) for L\u2081 projection on coarse grid\n     d. If improvement detected, interpolate candidate to fine grid\n\n3. CoarseGridDescent:\n   - For grid sizes N_d in [256,128,64]:\n     a. Start from best candidate from previous fine grid\n     b. Project onto N_d grid while preserving mass\n     c. Clamp boundaries and optimize using dominant eigenvector method\n     d. If C_5 improves on coarse grid, send to GridRefinement phase\n\n4. GridRefinement:\n   - Take best candidate from coarse grid and refine to finer resolutions\n   - Apply L-BFGS-B with carefully chosen boundaries\n   - Use Schur-complement projection to enforce constraints\n\n5. Optimization polishing (identical to original):\n   - SLSQP warmup, subgradient search, smooth gradient descent, noisy restarts, final SLSQP polish\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Coarse grid variations: Active set of grid sizes [256,128,64]: use 256 if time allows, else 128, etc.\n- Grid descent: Wearch (adaptive moment) method with reset parameter \u03bc=0.9\n- Transition between grids: time budget 10/3 ms per grid descent\n- Grid choice strategy: Weardescent (if initial time < 700s and grid size not attempted)\n- Constraint handling: Border-clamping for optimization\n- Objective evaluation: Fast full autocorrelation every 100th step\n\nComputation budget:\n- Phase1 (coarse grid): 4 grids, 200ms each -> 800ms\n- Phase2 (descent): 4 grids, 5thermalms each -> 200ms\n- Phase3 (refinement): 3 grids, 15ms each -> 45ms\n- Phase4 (polish): 1200ms (with budget of 1100ms)\n\nValidation plan:\n- For each candidate, validate:\n  1. Integral is within 1e-6 of 1.0\n  2. All entries are between 0 and 1 inclusive\n  3. C_5 computed using np.correlate then *dx\n- Lower is better comparison",
